day-09-act-03.answer:
Part A output:

```text
{'猫': 2, '犬': 1}
 M freq.py
{'猫': 2, '犬': 1}
{}
{'猫': 2, '犬': 1}
```

The appended line adds a print of the empty dictionary {}; git restore removes it, the status becomes empty, and the output returns to the committed version.

Part A alternative output:

```text
{'猫': 2, '犬': 1}
{'猫': 2, '犬': 1}
```

Copying the saved file back restores the exact bytes, so git status --short prints nothing.

Part B output:

```text
{'猫': 2, '犬': 1}
{'猫': 2, '犬': 1}
debug
Revert "Add debug print"
Add debug print
Add char_frequencies
{'猫': 2, '犬': 1}
```

The bad commit prints debug. git revert --no-edit HEAD adds a third commit, Revert "Add debug print". git diff HEAD~2 HEAD prints nothing, which proves the files match the original commit; the status is clean and the output is back to {'猫': 2, '犬': 1}.

Part C output:

```text
{'猫': 2, '犬': 1}
revert refused: uncommitted change present
 M freq.py
```

git revert refuses (exit code 128) because applying the inverse change would overwrite the uncommitted edit to freq.py; the edit is still there afterwards.
